    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p5-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the area of the largest regular hexagon that fits inside a circle of radius 1.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ square units.\n\nSplit the hexagon into six equilateral triangles of side 1.",
          "worked": "The largest hexagon has its vertices on the circle. Joining the centre to the vertices gives six equilateral triangles of side 1. Let $t$ be the height of one triangle.\n$$\\begin{align*}t^2&=1^2-\\left(\\frac12\\right)^2\\\\t^2&=\\frac34\\\\t&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\\\A&=6\\times\\frac12\\times1\\times\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\\\A&=\\frac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\end{align*}$$\nThe area is $\\dfrac{3\\sqrt3}{2}$ square units.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 82
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the area of the smallest regular hexagon that fits around a circle of radius 1.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2\\sqrt3$ square units.\n\nThe circle radius is the perpendicular height of each of the six central triangles.",
          "worked": "The smallest hexagon has each side tangent to the circle, so its apothem is 1. The six central triangles are equilateral. Let their side length be $s$.\n$$\\begin{align*}s^2&=1^2+\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)^2\\\\\\frac34s^2&=1\\\\s^2&=\\frac43\\\\s&=\\frac{2\\sqrt3}{3}\\\\A&=6\\times\\frac12\\times\\frac{2\\sqrt3}{3}\\times1\\\\A&=2\\sqrt3\\end{align*}$$\nThe area is $2\\sqrt3$ square units.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      }
    ]
  },
